<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fangtou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">369</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fangtou was fought in 369 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> defeated the northern expedition of the Eastern Jin commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> near Fangtou, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. Huan Wen's army, short of supplies, retreated and lost many thousands of men. The victory saved Former Yan for the moment, but court jealousy then drove Murong Chui to flee to the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the 310s, and Huan Wen, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in Sichuan in 347, reached the outskirts of Chang'an against the Former Qin in 354 and retook Luoyang in 356, but was badly defeated by the Former Yan at <b>Fangtou</b> in 369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Murong Ke died, court jealousy drove out the talented general Murong Chui, who had defeated Huan Wen at <b>Fangtou</b> in 369; he fled to Former Qin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a></li></ul></section>
